      "Language": "fre",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "Copyright © 2013 Elsevier Masson SAS. All rights reserved.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "OBJECTIVES",
            "content": "Identify parasitic myomas following uterine laparoscopic morcellation and describe the circumstances of diagnosis, management, potential consequences and possible preventive measures."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "Retrospective study of observed cases in a university hospital between 2000 and 2012 and review of the literature."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Five cases were identified in our department. Pelvic pain was the main symptom in three patients while one was asymptomatic and one consulted for a cystocele. The average time to diagnosis was 88 months (3-192). Surgical removal was performed in four cases by laparoscopy and vaginally for one case. Histological examination showed typical leiomyomas, but in one case, an atypical leiomyoma with limited experience for a typical primary lesion. In the literature, there are about 50 cases. One required a bowel resection and for another one, after subtotal hysterectomy, histological examination showed complex atypical endometrial hyperplasia for normal endometrium initially."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "This study should draw the attention of laparoscopic surgeon. It emphasizes, beyond a potential reoperation, a risk of atypical histological secondary processing. Surgical resection should be discussed even in case of asymptomatic lesions."
          }
        ]
      },
